// S~S  SimpleSal signal starts  S~S

// [Agency Events Defined.note]         search in ssTEA source files for a reference to this file.
//
   ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   A Time signal requests a grant of agency to an Event,
        which is done based on the OccursAt Time in relation to CurrentTime in Agency Root,
        continued agencying is a choice of the Event itself and Agency Root.

   A Data signal requests a grant of agency to an Event,
        which is done at the next occurence of Agency Root under the control of pace/period.
        A data Event is paused automatically, to prevent an Event running every Agency Root.
        To run an Event every pace/period, a 2nd Event (resumed by 1st) must resume 1st Event.

   A Ireq signal requests a grant of agency to an Event,
        which is done at the next occurence of Agency Root while ignoring pace/period.
        An Ireq Event is paused automatically, to prevent an Event running every Agency Root.
        To run an Event every Agency Root, a 2nd Ireq Event (resumed by 1st) must resume 1st.
        Ireq Events running every Agency Root is a "hot interrupt", not generally useful.
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Ireq and Data Events need take no action as to state except to return any "_ok_" return.
   Agency Run FSM state is set to paused on OK return, UNLESS Event paused or stopped itself.
   This fine point only matters when considering which statistic to update when paused.
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   In addition to that rule: Ireq and Data Events run for one Planck and then they are paused.
   This causes the Ireq Event Type to act like a hardware interrupt controller that requires
     interactions to change the state of the Interrupt Indication FSM within the controller.
     This is called the "interrupt acknowledgement"; the FSM readies the controller to re-fire.
   This causes the Data Event Type to act like a signal: the data exists between the Time
     the Data Event is signaled to resume and the Time the Data Event consumes the Data.
     A continuation of the signal would require more data, distinguishable from the first.
     The signal carrier in this case is the data allocated and pointed to by the sender.
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The result: Agency not allowed in the NEXT planck unless the Event's Agency Run FSM is
   resumed during THIS Planck.  An Ireq Event can run in the next Planck by signaling Resume
   to a Data Event that is otherwise ready to run in this Planck, as found by Agency Next.
   The Data Event's only task would be to Resume the Ireq Event, to run at the next Planck.
   -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// S~S  SimpleSal signal ends  S~S
